WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.200
Acetylcholine slows the heart.

2
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:08.740
The same acetylcholine makes the muscle around your airways contract.

3
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.040
Who decides the answer, the molecule or the cell?

4
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.080
The molecule carries no instruction about the answer.

5
00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:19.980
Each cell decides, through its receptor, its G protein and the proteins waiting

6
00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:20.980
downstream.

7
00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:26.050
This receptor is a G -protein coupled receptor, or GPCR.

8
00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:30.150
Its chain crosses the membrane seven times, as seven helices.

9
00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:34.830
Acetylcholine is an agonist, a ligand that activates its receptor.

10
00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.970
It binds in a pocket among the helices and favors the active shape.

11
00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:44.190
In that shape the inner end of helix 6 swings outward and opens a cavity.

12
00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:51.970
Waiting just inside the membrane is a G protein, and its Gα -alpha part holds GDP.

13
00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:58.150
Gβ -beta and Gγ -gamma bind each other so tightly that they act as one unit, beta

14
00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:03.570
-gamma. The active receptor grips the G protein and loosens alpha's hold on GDP.

15
00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:08.190
GDP leaves, and GTP takes the empty site.

16
00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:13.790
In the textbook picture, alpha with GTP then lets go of the receptor and of beta

17
00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:14.790
-gamma.

18
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:21.010
The cell has two messengers, alpha with GTP and free beta gamma, and each can find a

19
00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:25.570
target. In pacemaker cells of the heart, the receptor is M2.

20
00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.930
It couples to a G protein called Gi.

21
00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:34.910
Freed beta gamma opens a potassium channel, and potassium leaves, moving the voltage

22
00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:35.990
away from threshold.

23
00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:40.450
Alpha lowers cyclic AMP, cutting pacemaker current.

24
00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:41.970
The heart slows.

25
00:01:42.733 --> 00:01:45.413
In airway muscle, the receptor is M3.

26
00:01:45.993 --> 00:01:49.113
It couples to a different G protein, called Gq.

27
00:01:49.673 --> 00:01:53.693
Alpha with GTP switches on an enzyme, phospholipase C-β.

28
00:01:53.693 --> 00:01:56.513
-beta. The enzyme cuts PIP2.

29
00:01:57.013 --> 00:02:00.413
IP3 releases stored calcium, and the muscle contracts.

30
00:02:01.317 --> 00:02:06.377
In the heart, acetylcholine meets M2 and Gi, and the heart slows.

31
00:02:07.077 --> 00:02:11.257
In the airways, it meets M3 and Gq, and the muscle contracts.

32
00:02:12.057 --> 00:02:13.577
The acetylcholine is the same.

33
00:02:14.017 --> 00:02:17.177
The receptor and the G protein it couples to are different.

34
00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:22.937
So keep this rule: receptor, G protein and downstream proteins decide, not the

35
00:02:22.937 --> 00:02:23.237
signal.
